  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2$ and the point $P(1,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+4})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(x)=4x-4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{\u2033}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(1)^{2}-4(1)+6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-4+6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
